plan Inspection
title   Inspection: structural representation, live inspection, evidence, traces and redaction
status  proposed
goal
    An agent or a host can learn how an application is built and what it did without reading much
    code and without gaining execution authority. flow-state/inspect offers inspect, render,
    attachInspector and trace. Observation never starts work, never changes committed state,
    never persists implicitly, never invents evidence and never delays a turn. CLI leaves and
    flags are @plan.CommandLine.O1 to @plan.CommandLine.O5.
outcomes
    O1  inspect(app) and inspect(runtime)
        inspect(app) returns a Promise of a plain readonly Model. It loads every lazy entry
        (@plan.LazyFeatures.O5) and compiles the App without running any machine callback, guard,
        adapter, Layer or service; it builds no Runtime and touches no storage.

        The Model is read from each machine's compiled data view, whose closures are names
        (@plan.Runtime.O1). A failed load rejects with EntryLoadFailed naming the entry; a
        construction error rejects with the construction FlowError. Equal App code gives equal Model
        bytes on every host.

        The Model lists the app and entries (eager or lazy), each machine's schemas, states,
        transitions, guards with their possible deny codes, actions (each naming the operation it
        commits or reads), schedules, placements, outcome edges, context reads and persistence
        opt-ins; fragments, .each copies, fused transitions and contract summaries (fragment
        operations included) under their instance names; every operation with its contract, verb,
        concurrency, key fields and services; each entity domain as recorded (predicted field names,
        runtime markers and many, never functions; a guarded write's targets); the static actor-send
        graph and a fingerprint. It holds no live state, closures, Effects or services.

        A constant in a condition is a literal up to 256 bytes, otherwise its hash; a sensitive
        constant is never exported raw.

        inspect(runtime) is synchronous and passive. It reads the last committed cut and never a
        turn in progress, starts no work, adopts nothing, touches no storage and emits no evidence.
        It works on a constructed, ready, disposing or disposed Runtime. It returns frozen plain
        data with faithful in-process values: runtime lifecycle, clock and evidence sequence; work
        (each item with waitsFor); per actor its state, revision, mailbox depth, schedules,
        placements, fragment incarnations (status active or frozen), issues and uses ({ consumers,
        subscriptions, contexts, pendingEnsures, busy }, each subscription with its owner, busy
        listing work in flight by address, the mailbox depth, waiting deadlines and an unencodable
        record; a free actor shows its grace in work); write lanes by identity, bound or unbound,
        each with its operation path, subject path, binding (bound, unbound or unplaced), releasing
        mark, state (Idle, InFlight, Held), holds, queued commits, kept endings and parked endings,
        including quarantined lanes, old-version lanes and holds left after `disposeActor` or
        forget; resource entries and stream sessions (per session: state, drop, its registration row
        and basis, each proof, losses, bookmark and filled, bound and release, with counters and a
        ring of the last 256; flags ValuesWhileDown, RegistrationChurn, ReleaseOverdue,
        ProofWithoutPosition, PingsWithoutLive, IdleWithoutPing (proposed name; a Silent end of a
        connection with no ping since connect), DeliveryDenied, FillsUntouched and FillsOtherKey,
        proposed name); per domain the answers with frontier and stale causes, predictions, queues,
        uncertain records, given-up marks and a record wait as stalled: "record" (proposed);
        persistence status, including SecondTabNoPersistence; lazy entries adopted or not; hydration
        provenance.

        Held wire items in a Story are not Runtime facts.

        Actors are named by machine and input fields, Editor{workspaceId: "north", ticketId:
        "T-42"}, or a singleton's name.

        Revisions, timers and generations appear here and in evidence, never in public snapshots.

        flow explain is @plan.CommandLine.O1 and @plan.CommandLine.O4.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/tools/inspection.ts.
    O2  The structural codec
        One bounded codec carries every value that crosses a process or storage boundary: persisted
        records (@plan.Persistence.O3), server-rendering payloads (@plan.ServerRendering.O3), trace
        artifacts and the Model bytes. It accepts JSON values produced by the owning Schema.

        A value at a bound is accepted, one past it is rejected: depth 32, 10,000 nodes, 4,096 items
        per array, 262,144 UTF-8 bytes per string or key, 2,097,152 canonical bytes.

        Encoding is canonical: sorted keys, no whitespace, no -0, NaN, infinity, cycles, lone
        surrogates or sparse arrays. Decoding is strict: it rejects a byte-order mark, invalid
        UTF-8, and any input that does not re-encode to exactly the same bytes (duplicate keys,
        unsorted keys, odd number spellings), then decodes with the owning Schema.

        Equal accepted data gives equal bytes on every host; mutating an input or output byte array
        afterwards never changes any stored or decoded value.

        A failure is one FlowError raised before any actor, store, service or committed change:
        CodecBoundExceeded (naming the bound, its value and the path), CodecMalformed (with a
        reason, one of utf8, json, nonCanonical, cycle, negativeZero, nonFinite, loneSurrogate,
        notPlain or sparse) or CodecSchemaMismatch (path and issue summary, never data values).

        No raw parser error or Cause escapes. A value with no encoded form inside a turn never fails
        the turn: it is UnencodableValue (@plan.Operations.O3, @plan.Persistence.O5).
    O3  Inspector sinks
        attachInspector(runtime, callback) attaches a callback; attachInspector(runtime, { capacity?
        }) creates a bounded buffer. Both return drain() and dispose(); the buffer also returns
        snapshot() and clear().

        Capacity defaults to 256 and may be zero; an invalid capacity throws
        InvalidInspectorCapacity before attaching. snapshot() returns { records,
        truncatedBeforeSequence }, where the marker is null only if nothing was omitted, otherwise
        the greatest omitted or cleared sequence. Overflow drops the oldest records and advances the
        marker. clear() empties the window without resetting the sequence.

        A sink belongs to one Runtime and attaches once (InspectorAlreadyAttached on reuse);
        attaching to a disposed or disposing Runtime throws RuntimeDisposed. Attaching before
        ready() sees bootstrap evidence. A late attachment first gets a redacted current snapshot
        and a marker for what it missed, then later records only.

        Sinks never delay or change anything. Records are accepted with publication, delivered
        asynchronously in sequence order, and never run user code inside a turn. A slow sink never
        blocks turns, sends, context waves or persistence.

        A callback that throws detaches only itself; drain and dispose reject with
        InspectorSinkFailed and a diagnostic reaches healthy sinks. drain() waits for the evidence
        accepted when it was called; dispose() delivers that prefix, detaches, is idempotent, and no
        callback runs after it resolves. Runtime disposal accepts terminal lifecycle evidence for
        consumers before providers. No omission is silent and no buffer is persisted. Reference:
        references/dispatch-desk/tools/inspection.ts.
    O4  Traces
        Every Runtime decision is an evidence event with one Runtime-global sequence starting at 1,
        so events of every kind order against each other; every event carries that run-wide sequence
        and, in a Story, tab: { name, open }.

        A committed turn's events have contiguous sequences and are accepted with the commit; a
        rolled-back turn leaves one turn event with result "failed" and its code.

        An event is delivered only after the state it describes is published. Lifecycle events are
        not turns and never advance an actor revision. When sequence headroom runs out, admission
        refuses before any commit.

        Event kinds cover machines (turn, which carries the keys its mappers read that it writes,
        with the alternative each part took; lifecycle; life, each actor end and return, the end
        naming the live work it stopped and a create within 5 s of an end how long ago; admission;
        send; schedule; context; callback; exit; listRepair; drop; issue; diagnostic), operations
        (resource, ending, which names operation, contract, verb and key; lane, with its cell;
        session, with its cell; delivery), entity domains (delta, field, repair, skip, lookup,
        prediction, predictionFault, queue, uncertain, answer, invalidate, tombstone, selfCheck,
        audit), persistence (capture, write, flush, restored, persistence-lock), lazy entries
        (entry-adopted, entry-adoption-failed) and server rendering (dehydrate, hydrate); Story runs
        add step, request, control and verdict.

        A Story's records take 21 kinds in one list. Evidence kinds include Superseded, Released,
        NothingToResend and InFlight; queue removals carry their reason (Superseded, Cancelled,
        Released); uncertain's end reads "a later write succeeded, or a retry of the Attempt
        succeeded" as amended by @plan.EntityDomains.O1; UnusedWireRule is a Story-derived
        diagnostic.

        Boot reports and evidence named by the owner plans appear here: TurnStorm, StorageSilent,
        OldVersionLane and Stale, and, all proposed names, RecordUndecodable, UnboundLane,
        LaneRekeyed, OpRenamed, LaneRekeyCollision, HoldWithoutRecord, HoldForgotten,
        RecordShapeChanged, FieldDropped, RecordHeld, RepairOverdue, UnsettledEvicted,
        GuardContradicted and SelectedNoKey.

        A second tab emits persistence-lock with SecondTabNoPersistence and no capture or restore;
        no multi-tab coordination events exist in v1.

        A Story run produces a Trace (@plan.Testing.O4); a production Runtime exports no trace, and
        hosts keep redacted sink events in their own logs. flow trace <story> [--at <checkpoint>]
        prints a Story's timeline (@plan.CommandLine.O2). trace.export(trace) returns canonical
        bytes and trace.import(bytes) validates them and returns a Trace; both are synchronous and
        need no App.

        A Trace holds kind and version, `storyId`, `appId`, `appFingerprint`, seed, a
        `truncatedBeforeSequence` marker, redacted events, uniquely named redacted checkpoints,
        outcome ("completed" or "failed"), end, failure and cleanup status. completed requires an
        end; a failure before end capture leaves end null. It keeps at most 4,096 recent events,
        dropping the oldest until it fits the codec bounds and advancing the marker; checkpoints are
        never dropped, and if they cannot fit the export fails with CodecBoundExceeded.

        Import validates in order and rejects with one FlowError:

        - the codec;
        - kind, version and a closed schema (unknown or omitted members reject with
          InvalidTraceRecord);
        - identity fields (appId, appFingerprint form);
        - strictly increasing sequences consistent with the marker;
        - lane and field correlations (Ready carries data, unknown carries its reason and an attempt
          id);
        - lifecycle only active, then disposing, then disposed per incarnation
          (InvalidLifecycleTransition);
        - and outcome consistency, so failed cleanup never yields completed (InvalidStoryEvidence).

        A Trace never contains callbacks, Effects, Layers, live refs, raw Cause, stacks or fiber
        ids, and is never a restore, hydrate or Story input. Reference:
        references/dispatch-desk/tools/inspection.ts (loadTrace).
    O5  Redaction and digests
        In-process reads, checkpoints, snapshots and inspect(runtime) carry raw values. Redaction
        applies where data leaves the process: sink events, trace artifacts and CLI output.
        Server-rendering payloads omit sensitive data entirely (@plan.ServerRendering.O3).

        A value under a Schema annotated sensitive: true, or a Schema.Redacted node, at any depth of
        an event, input, memory, context, key, output, error, stream value or entity field, leaves
        the process as "hmac:" plus 64 hex characters of HMAC-SHA-256 over its canonical bytes.
        Equal values under one key give equal digests, but a dictionary cannot recover them without
        the key.

        The key is 32 random bytes per Runtime, never exported or persisted. In Stories it is a
        private per-run key, reused when the same run repeats so repeated traces are identical, and
        not derivable from the public seed. A value that cannot be encoded as JSON is carried as {
        unencodable: <Schema identifier> }, never its contents.

        In artifacts, every entity and resource value, sensitive or not, appears only as field names
        and digests, so data never leaves through a trace; a Story's fake truth and request inputs
        leave the process as digests. Identities, versions, kinds, sequences, state paths, event
        tags and codes are never redacted, nor is a request's via, which is an identity.
    O6  Renderers
        render(model, "text" | "json" | "mermaid") is synchronous and deterministic. Text prints
        each machine's states, transitions, guards with deny codes, outcome edges, schedules and
        sends, and each domain as declaration sentences, with lazy entries marked. json is the
        canonical Model. mermaid draws a state diagram per machine and a flowchart of sends and
        context reads.

        Trace projections (printed by @plan.CommandLine.O4) read one or two Traces and run no
        application code. summary gives counts and a per-actor timeline always stating "complete" or
        "truncated before N" and the outcome.

        The sections are a closed list of normalized events by kind: transitions, sends, schedules,
        lifecycle, resources (with why each refetched), write lanes and endings, repairs, restore,
        hydration, adoption and diagnostics. Filters are data: actor, attempt id, read, entity key
        with sequence, issues only.

        A selector naming something absent from the app's Model fails with InvalidArtifactOperand
        (reason UnknownSelector); one known to the Model but absent from the trace answers none when
        the trace is complete. Under truncation, absence is reported as "insufficient evidence"
        (EvidenceUnavailable), never "none".

        Comparing two Traces has four results: equal (both complete, sections match), different
        (a retained fact differs, even if truncated), incomplete (retained facts match and an
        input is truncated), and the failure ArtifactIdentityMismatch when appId, appFingerprint
        or format version differ. coverage lists the declared transitions, deny codes, outcome
        edges, schedules and sends that no given Trace exercised.
decisions
    D1  The machine file is the spec
        The Model is the only structural representation; graph, diff, explain, check, render and
        the fingerprint all read it. No generated spec or saved structure file exists. The domain
        lint's reading of source is a check, not a representation; it is never saved.
    D2  Deny codes are read from types, never by running guards
        The compiler reads each guard's literal return type. If complete facts are unavailable,
        inspect(app) rejects with TypeFactsUnavailable instead of returning an empty set as
        exhaustive. The type-facts reader is one adapter, also used by the domain lint, and fails
        closed for both. The compiler stays out of application bundles.
    D3  The fingerprint covers the whole Model
        appFingerprint is SHA-256 of the canonical Model excluding itself and run data, so any
        change to lazy content or deny codes changes it. There is no public fingerprint API.
    D4  Revisions are per incarnation and survive restore
        A new incarnation starts at 0; a restored or hydrated actor resumes its captured
        revision, so evidence compares across a reload.
constraints
    C1  Observation is never authority
        No mutable trace log, implicit history, fabricated trace, sink-owned actor work, snapshot
        replay or trace-driven restore exists; records become durable only through an explicit
        trace export. Artifact schemas are closed, and a format change bumps the version with no
        legacy importer.
    C2  Inspection stays out of application bundles
        flow-state, flow-state/react and flow-state/persistence never import flow-state/inspect,
        the compiler or a renderer.
verification
    V1  Model: a golden Model of the reference app; a spy shows no callback, adapter or Layer
        ran; a lazy variant equals the eager one except for loading; equal bytes on Node and a
        browser.
    V2  inspect(runtime): during a turn it returns the previous cut, never mixes revisions,
        starts no work, touches no storage and leaves the sequence unchanged; lanes by identity,
        uses and incarnations match the Runtime.
    V3  Codec: each bound at its value and one past it; malformed UTF-8, BOM, lone surrogates,
        duplicate keys, -0 and non-canonical spellings fail with the right code and path; caller
        mutation of bytes changes nothing.
    V4  Sinks: a stalled sink never delays turns; a failing sink detaches alone; capacity 0, 1
        and default; attach before ready and late attach; exact markers; drain and dispose
        accepted-prefix behaviour.
    V5  Events and traces: one Story per event kind; contiguous turn sequences; a rolled-back
        turn leaves one failed event; export then import is byte-identical; every invalid import
        vector is rejected with its code; no Cause or stack appears in any artifact.
    V6  Redaction: sensitive values are digests in sink events and exported artifacts,
        checkpoints included, and raw in in-process checkpoints; no sensitive value is found in
        any artifact; digests are equal under one key and not recoverable by a dictionary without
        it.
    V7  Renderers and projections: golden text, json and mermaid; every section and filter; the
        four comparison results; "insufficient evidence" under truncation; coverage.
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        - Exact event field lists;
        - counters per turn;
        - Model field layout;
        - mermaid id derivation;
        - domain-literal hashing;
        - sequence headroom reservation;
        - fingerprint ordering rules;
        - supplying type facts outside source tooling.
